Right.  From my perspective, if I'm not using the hat of smart, the +5
is the only remaining significant property... along with the 'iron' so
things hitting me on the head don't hurt.  That's only something I care
about on early levels anyway, really.

> But not the enchantment, the telepathy is what is invaluable; you
> can plan in advance and are less susceptible to fatal surprises if
> you have it. IMO it's the most desirable helm until the endgame.[*]

Invaluable?  Hardly.  There are no fatal surprises for a properly played
character, as there's almost nothing left that can hurt them.  And I'm
not talking about a massively overprepped character, either, I'm talking
about your plain old run-of-the-mill 200HP, -20AC guy.

Mind flayer?  Elbereth.  Arch-lich?  Elbereth.  Summon storm?  Elbereth.
Archon, titan?  Teleport away, ^T to stairs, leave level and threat
behind forever. 

This is even assuming you haven't genocided L and h, which is a pretty
common approach among people who are Trying To Win(tm).

If I get the hat of think before I get around to enchanting my helm,
I'll switch out, of course.  But once the hat's +4 or +5, I'm not
swapping it out for +0 telepathy.  It just isn't necessary or even
particularly that helpful -- I certainly am not going to wish, polypile,
or otherwise make any effort requiring resources to obtain one.

> Interesting again; even with spellcasters I do prefer wearing the
> robe (if I can get one); the brilliance effect seems (at least to
> failure rates) much less effective.

The robe's bonus is directly to spell success.  The brilliance bonus is
solely for INT and WIS, and I don't usually find many gain ability
potions, so this ends up mattering for my non-wizard casters.  Ideally I
use both hat-of-smart _and_ robe.  :)

> Unless you have other means of permanent telepathy like the spell
> at sufficient proficiency or the amulet if you have reflection by
> other means.

I can't recall ever casting clairvoyance.  I've worn the amulet of ESP
before, (my personal preference is reflection, ESP, life saving in that
order) but in my last few games I tended to just bolt on life saving 
and not worry about it.  Still never used one*, though, which is why the
order of preference above hasn't changed.

* I can't remember having ever used one.  NAO's records show that I've
never lifesaved, which somewhat bears it out, but I can't really speak
too confidently about, say, ten years ago, of course.

